<b>And they shall return the overreaching.</b> See Beurim: this applies whether the orphans were overcharged or the creditor was overcharged. If the overreaching was one sixth, regarding the period within which one normally may show the transaction to another merchant, there is no distinction whether that period has passed or not. See Beurim. The rule stated here does not apply where the debtor personally took possession of land or movables according to beit din's appraisal. But if bidders added above the appraisal and the creditor himself added too much, the transaction remains valid despite the overreaching in his voluntary added bid. If the appraisers themselves erred in the original appraisal by one sixth, the transaction is void.
